     The following resources are consumed by lliibbssaa - stack, heap, console and
     devices.

     The stack must be established before lliibbssaa functions can be invoked.
     Stack requirements vary depending on the functions and file systems used
     by the consumer and the support layer functions detailed below.

     The heap must be established before calling aalllloocc() or ooppeenn() by calling
     sseetthheeaapp().  Heap usage will vary depending on the number of simultane‐
     ously open files, as well as client behaviour.  Automatic decompression
     will allocate more than 64K of data per open file.

     Console access is performed via the ggeettcchhaarr(), ppuuttcchhaarr() and iisscchhaarr()
     functions detailed below.

     Device access is initiated via ddeevvooppeenn() and is performed through the
     ddvv__ssttrraatteeggyy(), ddvv__iiooccttll() and ddvv__cclloossee() functions in the device switch
     structure that ddeevvooppeenn() returns.

     The consumer must provide the following support functions:

     _i_n_t ggeettcchhaarr(_v_o_i_d)

                 Return a character from the console, used by ggeettss(), nnggeettss()
                 and pager functions.

     _i_n_t iisscchhaarr(_v_o_i_d)

                 Returns nonzero if a character is waiting from the console.

     _v_o_i_d ppuuttcchhaarr(_i_n_t)

                 Write a character to the console, used by ggeettss(), nnggeettss(),
                 **pprriinnttff(), ppaanniicc() and ttwwiiddddllee() and thus by many other func‐
                 tions for debugging and informational output.

     _i_n_t ddeevvooppeenn(_s_t_r_u_c_t _o_p_e_n___f_i_l_e _*_o_f, _c_o_n_s_t _c_h_a_r _*_n_a_m_e, _c_o_n_s_t _c_h_a_r _*_*_f_i_l_e)

                 Open the appropriate device for the file named in _n_a_m_e, re‐
                 turning in _f_i_l_e a pointer to the remaining body of _n_a_m_e which
                 does not refer to the device.  The _f___d_e_v field in _o_f will be
                 set to point to the _d_e_v_s_w structure for the opened device if
                 successful.  Device identifiers must always precede the path
                 component, but may otherwise be arbitrarily formatted.  Used
                 by ooppeenn() and thus for all device-related I/O.

